{"step_index":259,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-08T21:26:44Z","input_tokens":2549,"cache_read_tokens":182631,"output_tokens":608,"content":"In web development, notes are called **comments**. Browsers ignore them when running your code:\n\n### 1. HTML\nWrap your note inside `<!--` and `-->`:\n```html\n<!-- This is a note in HTML -->\n<div class=\"card\">...</div>\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. CSS (inside `<style>` or `.css` files)\nWrap your note inside `/*` and `*/`:\n```css\n/* Single line note */\n.card {\n  background: var(--card); /* Inline note */\n}\n\n/*\n  Multi-line note:\n  This can span several lines.\n*/\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. JavaScript (inside `<script>` or `.js` files)\n- **Single-line note**: Use two forward slashes `//`:\n  ```javascript\n  // This is a single-line note\n  const score = 100; // You can also put it after code\n  ```\n- **Multi-line note**: Wrap inside `/*` and `*/`:\n  ```javascript\n  /*\n    This is a multi-line note\n    explaining complex logic.\n  */\n  function calculate() { ... }\n  ```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Keyboard Shortcut in Most Code Editors (VS Code, etc.):**\n> Highlight any line(s) and press `Ctrl + /` (or `Cmd + /` on Mac) to automatically comment or uncomment that line.","thinking":"The query concerns adding notes to code, specifically HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Rule 9 prohibits adding comments or documentation directly within the code. Considering the behavioral constraints is important to provide an appropriate response.\n\n\n"}
